<Project>

  <!--
    Location-independent builds (release audit R3/F2).

    Roslyn's deterministic compilation hashes the SOURCE FILE PATHS into the assembly, so
    the same commit produced a different application DLL — and therefore a different
    single-file bundle ID and EXE — from every clone directory. The audit measured exactly
    that: two clones agreed with each other but not with a package built elsewhere, and two
    further clones (clone-c / clone-d) disagreed again. A controlled experiment confirmed
    the cause: rebuilding twice in the SAME directory yields a byte-identical DLL, while a
    different directory does not.

    PathMap rewrites those embedded paths to a fixed virtual root, so the compilation sees
    the same paths no matter where the repository is checked out. It applies to every
    project in the repository (including generated sources under obj\, which live inside
    the mapped root) so the whole graph is consistent.

    Note the remaining scope limit, which is documented rather than hidden: this makes the
    build location-independent on a pinned toolchain. Paths OUTSIDE the repository — the
    NuGet package cache and the SDK — are not mapped, so cross-machine reproduction still
    requires the frozen build environment listed as an open release item.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PathMap>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)=/_/</PathMap>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
